REGIONAL REGULATION OF PAKPAK BHARAT REGENCY

NUMBER 2 OF 2017

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF PAKPAK BHARAT,

Considering:

a. that discrimination and violence against women and children constitute violations of human rights and crimes against humanity;
b. that in order to fulfill the constitutional rights of women and children and improve the quality of life, it is necessary to regulate the duties, authorities, and responsibilities of the Regional Government in the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence.

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 9 of 2003 concerning the Establishment of South Nias Regency, Pakpak Bharat Regency and Humbang Hasundutan Regency in the Province of North Sumatra (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4272);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 33 of 2004 concerning Financial Balance Between the Central Government and Regional Governments (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 126, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4438);
7. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
8. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
9. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
10. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
11. Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
12. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2007 concerning the Coordination Forum for the Implementation of Cooperation on Prevention and Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence;
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning Implementation of Women's Protection;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Implementation of Child Protection;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Child Victims of Violence;
18. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence.

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PAKPAK BHARAT REGENCY

and

REGENT OF PAKPAK BHARAT

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of women and children victims of violence is organized based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. non-discriminatory;
d. protection of victims' rights;
e. legal certainty.

Article 3

1. The purpose of organizing protection of women and children victims of violence is to:
a. prevent acts of violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect, provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters and witnesses;
e. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
2. Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic neglect;
e. restriction of movement.

CHAPTER III

VICTIMS' RIGHTS

Article 4

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to obtain:
a. respect for dignity and worth as a human being;
b. protection from family, community, Regional Government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the court;
c. health services according to medical needs;
d. special handling related to victim confidentiality;
e. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. spiritual guidance services;
g. to determine their own decisions.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 5

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to women and children victims of violence are the joint responsibility of:
a. regional government;
b. community;
c. family;
d. parents.

Article 6

1. The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to provide protection to women and children victims of violence.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the regional government as referred to in Article 5 letter a include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence based on laws and regulations;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence;
d. facilitating assistance, legal aid and legal services according to the needs of victims;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from victims of violence according to regional financial capacity; and;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence.
3. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan as a basis for OPD in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
4. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (3), is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 5 letter b, are carried out in the form of community participation.
2. The forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. preventing acts of violence against women and children;
b. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to law enforcement or authorized parties;
c. participating in handling victims of acts of violence;
d. providing protection to victims; and
e. providing emergency assistance.

CHAPTER V

PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 9

1. To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the regional government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 10

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 9, is carried out by OPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. health;
b. education;
c. social affairs;
d. women's empowerment and child protection;
e. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by OPD as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on the regional action plan.

CHAPTER VI

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 11

1. In the case of implementing protection for women and children victims of violence, the regional government may establish an Integrated Service Center (PPT).
2. The establishment and development of PPT is adjusted to the development of priority needs, and regional financial capacity as well as the institutional capacity of personnel in the region.
3. PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) may accommodate institutions/work units that provide services for victims of violence such as P2TP2A, Safe Houses, Child Social Protection Houses, Trauma Center Houses, Women's Social Protection Houses, Shelters and other service activities in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
4. The forms of services implemented by PPT, include:
a. complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. legal aid;
e. repatriation;
f. social reintegration.
5. In providing protection to women and children victims of violence, PPT is obligated to:
a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to victims;
b. organize protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal aid;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for victims;
d. network with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperate with witness and victim protection institutions, social protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for the health recovery of victims;
e. provide convenience, comfort, and safety for victims;
f. maintain the confidentiality of victims;
g. provide fulfillment of legal aid for victims;
h. in the case that integrated service implementation is carried out through networking, PPT remains responsible for the entire referral process of services needed by victims.
6. The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (3) is implemented in accordance with SPM.

CHAPTER VII

MINIMUM SERVICE STANDARDS

Article 12

SPM in the field of integrated services for women and children victims of violence, includes services for:
a. handling complaints/reports of victims of violence against women and children;
b. health services for women and children victims of violence;
c. social rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;
d. law enforcement and legal aid for women and children victims of violence;
e. repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of violence.

Article 13

SPM as referred to in Article 12 has performance indicators including:
a. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive complaint handling, health services, social rehabilitation services, spiritual guidance services conducted by trained officers within the integrated service unit;
b. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of violence against women and children;
c. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive legal aid services;
d. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; and
e. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.

CHAPTER XII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 19

In implementing protection for women and children victims of violence, the community may:
a. establish family partners at the village level by the community;
b. conduct independent socialization of the rights of women and children;
c. provide first aid to victims;
d. participate with the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
e. community participation shall be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.
